1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,820
Right now there's a zombie pressed up
against my gate, and it's wearing my best

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,090
friend's uniform.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,170
Behind him, the hallway is full of them.

4
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:15,230
The radio on his chest is about to tell
every one of them exactly where I'm hiding.

5
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:20,430
An hour ago, I was basically
the king of this mall.

6
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:21,850
Let me back up.

7
00:00:25,510 --> 00:00:28,630
I have a key to every store in this mall.

8
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:32,950
The man who gave them to me
said never to use the last one.

9
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:34,420
I'm Danny.

10
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:38,980
I do night security at Maplecrest Mall,
and yeah, I've heard every mall cop joke

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,000
there is.

12
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:44,840
I work nights so I can be at the bus stop
at three-fifteen when my daughter Lily

13
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:45,720
gets off.

14
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:47,270
My shift is nine to five.

15
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:52,520
Mostly I walk, I check doors, and I eat the
pretzels the food court guy was going to

16
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:53,540
throw out anyway.

17
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:56,450
She's seven, and she thinks
I'm the king of the mall.

18
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:00,590
She drew me a picture once, me
in a crown, holding up my keys.

19
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,890
It's taped to the monitor
in the security office.

20
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:07,480
We live with my mom, on the tenth floor of
the Parkview Apartments, right across the

21
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,500
highway.

22
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:13,920
Every night at nine, when I walk in to
work, Lily blinks her lamp at the mall three

23
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:14,800
times.

24
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:15,870
It means I love you.

25
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:17,410
I blink my flashlight back.

26
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,750
I taught her one more signal,
S-O-S, for emergencies.

27
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,090
She thinks it's a game.

28
00:01:22,170 --> 00:01:24,570
I really hope she never has to use it.

29
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:26,220
Forty-two keys.

30
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:30,540
Old Walt had this job before me, and when
he handed them over, he tapped the last

31
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:31,560
one.

32
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:34,390
"That one, you never use," he said.

33
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:35,940
"Don't ask."

34
00:01:36,122 --> 00:01:38,190
Its tag says B3.

35
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:40,610
There is no B3 on the mall map.

36
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:42,910
Tonight, I'd only need one of them.

37
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:46,050
In thirty days, I'd find
out what the last one opens.

38
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:51,860
Every night at one a.m., my best friend
tells me a terrible joke on the radio.

39
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:53,470
Tonight, he whispered.

40
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:55,850
Marcus guards the parking garage.

41
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:59,930
He's twenty-two, he's studying to be a
nurse, and he still owes me twenty bucks.

42
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,040
He lost a bet on a football game back in
September, and he's been dodging me ever

43
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:04,940
since.

44
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:08,330
At one on the dot, the radio clicks.

45
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:09,600
[MARCUS] Danny.

46
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:12,350
Why don't zombies ever eat clowns?

47
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:14,270
[MARCUS] Because they taste funny.

48
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:16,270
He laughs at his own jokes.

49
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:18,100
Every single time.

50
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:21,230
At one-forty, the radio clicks again.

51
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:22,520
[MARCUS] Danny.

52
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:23,930
There's a guy on the ramp.

53
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:25,650
He's walking... wrong.

54
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:28,250
Marcus doesn't whisper. Ever.

55
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:29,340
[MARCUS] Sir?

56
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:30,530
Sir, are you okay?

57
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:31,860
You're bleeding...

58
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:35,190
Then the radio did something
I'd never heard it do.

59
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:36,910
It went completely silent.

60
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:39,740
I said his name into the radio.

61
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,150
Then I said it again.

62
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:41,990
Nothing.

63
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:46,730
The security office has a wall of screens,
one for every camera in the building.

64
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:51,380
In six years, the most exciting thing
I've seen on them is a raccoon in the food

65
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:52,160
court.

66
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:52,780
Twice.

67
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:53,740
Same raccoon.

68
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,270
I pulled up camera six.

69
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:57,050
The garage, level two.

70
00:02:57,280 --> 00:03:02,350
Marcus was on the ground, and the
man from the ramp was on top of him.

71
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,270
Behind them, more were coming
up the ramp from the highway.

72
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:07,260
Six.

73
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:07,980
Ten.

74
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Thirty.

75
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:10,000
They weren't running.

76
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:14,890
They just walked, all at the same slow
speed, like a crowd leaving a stadium.

77
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:16,840
[MARCUS] Danny...

78
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:18,210
don't come down.

79
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:19,680
Lock up.

80
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:21,730
I'd like to tell you I ran down there.

81
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:22,380
I didn't.

82
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,390
I stood in that office with my
hand on the door, and I watched.

83
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,040
Then I picked up the phone
and called nine-one-one.

84
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:33,250
It rang and rang, and then a
recording said all circuits were busy.

85
00:03:33,430 --> 00:03:35,890
My mom's phone went straight to voicemail.

86
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:38,370
So did the next nine calls.

87
00:03:38,780 --> 00:03:42,690
And then every one of them on
that screen turned the same way.

88
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:44,410
Not toward the street.

89
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:45,520
Toward the mall.

90
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:49,170
One camera after another,
I watched them come inside.

91
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:50,280
The loading dock.

92
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:51,630
The service hall.

93
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:53,070
The doors by the restrooms.

94
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:57,240
The only screen that stayed black was
camera fourteen, the hallway behind the food

95
00:03:57,260 --> 00:03:58,180
court.

96
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:59,550
It had been out since Tuesday.

97
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:00,660
I'd put in a ticket.

98
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:03,050
They were all heading for the food court.

99
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,390
And the food court is made of glass.

100
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:07,810
I did the math.

101
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:10,970
At the speed they were walking,
I had about four minutes.

102
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:13,960
Four minutes to pick the
one store I'd survive in.

103
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:14,990
Which one would you pick?

104
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:16,530
Here's the thing about malls.

105
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:18,510
They're built to let people in.

106
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:19,780
That's the whole point of them.

107
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,800
But every store has a steel gate.

108
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:23,650
And I have every key.

109
00:04:23,780 --> 00:04:26,750
The pharmacy has medicine.
It's also all glass.

110
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:30,750
The food court has food, and
that's exactly where they're going.

111
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:34,470
The department store has beds,
and nine different entrances.

112
00:04:34,685 --> 00:04:40,170
And Summit Outdoors has tents, water
filters, baseball bats, and one way in.

113
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:42,130
Every night guard has a zombie plan.

114
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:43,550
Mine was Summit Outdoors.

115
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:45,510
I just never thought I'd actually use it.

116
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,790
Mall cops aren't exactly
famous for running.

117
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:48,300
I ran.

118
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:53,240
Down the escalator, past the fountain, past
the pretzel place, with the keys already

119
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:54,070
in my hand.

120
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:57,160
I was ten feet from the
gate when I heard it.

121
00:04:57,180 --> 00:04:59,950
Behind me, the food court doors exploded.

122
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,010
Key thirty-one. Under the
gate, pull it down, lock it.

123
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:07,670
I've done that a thousand times.

124
00:05:08,030 --> 00:05:10,210
I have never done it that fast.

125
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:16,230
Then I sat down in the dark, between
the tents, with my hand over my mouth.

126
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:18,860
Through the gate, I could hear
them filling up the hallway.

127
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:20,110
Shuffling.

128
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:21,330
Bumping into things.

129
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:24,250
Dozens of feet that never
quite left the floor.

130
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:27,170
I did the breathing thing
Lily learned in second grade.

131
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:28,290
In for four.

132
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:29,590
Out for four.

133
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:34,250
Once, something leaned on my
gate, and the whole thing groaned.

134
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:35,460
Then it moved on.

135
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:38,410
Lily's drawing was still
up in the security office.

136
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:42,930
I remember thinking, the king
of the mall is hiding in a tent.

137
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:44,500
Twenty minutes went by.

138
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:45,390
Then thirty.

139
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:47,590
I started to think maybe that was it.

140
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:52,570
They'd wander off, and in the morning it'd
be on the news, and I'd be a funny story.

141
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:53,970
And then somebody knocked.

142
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:55,080
One knock.

143
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:56,210
Two quick ones.

144
00:05:56,260 --> 00:05:57,060
One more.

145
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:57,760
Our knock.

146
00:05:57,780 --> 00:05:59,810
The one Marcus and I
use on the office door.

147
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:00,560
Danny.

148
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:01,320
It's me.

149
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:03,030
He knew which store I'd pick.

150
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:04,080
Of course he did.

151
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:07,330
I turned on my flashlight for one second.

152
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:09,050
His face was gray.

153
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:12,490
And his whole sleeve was dark and wet.

154
00:06:12,650 --> 00:06:14,690
I had the key in my hand.

155
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:17,810
Come on, man.

156
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:18,700
Open it.

157
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:19,640
Please.

158
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:21,770
There was a bite on his arm.

159
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:23,310
I could see it in the light.

160
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:26,840
And past him, at the end of the
hall, there were dozens of them.

161
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:27,860
Standing still.

162
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:28,580
Listening.

163
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,250
If I opened that gate,
they'd all come in with him.

164
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:34,480
My hand was shaking so hard
the keys were rattling.

165
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:37,010
I had to press them against
my chest to make them stop.

166
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:38,970
Marcus watched my face.

167
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:40,570
And he got it before I did.

168
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:41,340
Hey.

169
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,370
It's okay.

170
00:06:42,470 --> 00:06:43,870
I wouldn't open it either.

171
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:49,380
He sat down with his back against the
bars, and I sat down on my side, and we

172
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:51,170
whispered for forty minutes.

173
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:53,410
About stupid stuff.

174
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:54,440
The Bills.

175
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:55,530
His mom's lasagna.

176
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:59,040
The time he got locked in a fitting room
at the department store and I had to come

177
00:06:59,060 --> 00:06:59,900
get him out.

178
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:01,370
I'm not gonna lie, man.

179
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:02,690
I'm kind of scared.

180
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:04,040
So was I.

181
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:07,230
I told him I was right here,
and I wasn't going anywhere.

182
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:08,400
Which was true.

183
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:09,730
That was the whole problem.

184
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:12,510
Tell Lily the king's still in his castle.

185
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:16,670
Then, because he's Marcus, he
told me one more terrible joke.

186
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,790
What's a zombie's favorite kind of bean?

187
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:21,870
A human bean.

188
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,330
It wasn't funny.

189
00:07:23,740 --> 00:07:26,650
I laughed so hard I had to
put my face in a sleeping bag.

190
00:07:26,844 --> 00:07:27,680
And Danny?

191
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:29,410
I still owe you twenty bucks.

192
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:32,610
At two thirty-one,
Marcus stopped breathing.

193
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,710
At two thirty-nine, he stood up.

194
00:07:36,890 --> 00:07:41,210
He didn't look at me. He just stood
there with his face against the bars.

195
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:43,690
Two forty-five in the morning.

196
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:45,230
That's where you came in.

197
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:47,470
Then my radio crackled.

198
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:49,210
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] Is
anyone still in the mall?

199
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:52,270
I'm on the second floor
of the department store.

200
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:53,640
Please.

201
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:54,540
I'm not alone up here.

202
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:55,340
A woman.

203
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,220
Whispering.

204
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:57,930
Somewhere upstairs in my mall.

205
00:07:58,100 --> 00:08:00,110
Her voice came out of my radio.

206
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:03,560
And then, one second later, out
of the radio on Marcus's chest.

207
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:05,970
Every head in that hallway turned.

208
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:07,730
Forty of them. One gate.

209
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:14,020
They didn't run at it. They leaned on it,
all at once, the way a crowd leans on the

210
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:15,630
doors on Black Friday.

211
00:08:15,825 --> 00:08:17,410
And the steel started to bend.

212
00:08:17,648 --> 00:08:19,190
And she was about to call again.

213
00:08:20,330 --> 00:08:21,980
I switched my radio off.

214
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,450
But his was still on.

215
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:25,540
There was only one way to shut it up.

216
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:28,710
I had to reach through the
bars and take it off his chest.

217
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,020
His teeth were about
an inch from my wrist.

218
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:33,650
The whole time, he was
looking right at me.

219
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:35,050
And the clip stuck.

220
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:37,750
He always wore it upside down.

221
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:39,590
I must have told him a hundred times.

222
00:08:40,310 --> 00:08:44,520
I twisted it, and it came loose, and
I fell back on the floor with it.

223
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:49,660
I stuffed it as deep into a sleeping
bag as it would go, and I lay there, not

224
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:50,500
breathing.

225
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:52,950
Without the noise, they forgot about me.

226
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:54,330
It took maybe ten minutes.

227
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:57,010
One by one, they drifted
back down the hall.

228
00:08:57,253 --> 00:08:58,110
All except Marcus.

229
00:08:58,130 --> 00:09:02,170
Then, from inside the sleeping
bag, she said my name.

230
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:07,050
When I grabbed that radio, I must
have squeezed the talk button.

231
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:08,280
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] Danny, right?

232
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:09,220
The night guard.

233
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:10,810
I can hear you breathing.

234
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:13,570
She didn't sound scared anymore.

235
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:16,290
She sounded like someone
ordering a coffee.

236
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:17,440
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] Don't worry.

237
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:18,610
I'm not one of them.

238
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:20,890
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] You've
got all the keys, don't you?

239
00:09:21,450 --> 00:09:22,690
I didn't say a word.

240
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:23,940
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] That's okay.

241
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:25,070
Get some sleep, Danny.

242
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:26,010
We'll talk tomorrow.

243
00:09:26,030 --> 00:09:29,270
Then the little light
on the radio went out.

244
00:09:29,690 --> 00:09:30,710
I didn't sleep.

245
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:34,840
I lay there and listened to Marcus lean
on the gate every few minutes, like he was

246
00:09:34,860 --> 00:09:36,270
checking that it was still locked.

247
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:37,670
Here's what bothered me.

248
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:39,230
The mall closes at nine.

249
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:43,630
At midnight, I walked every hallway and
checked every door, like I do every night.

250
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:45,310
The department store was locked.

251
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:46,360
I locked it.

252
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:47,570
Nobody was in here.

253
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:49,020
So who was she?

254
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:51,610
And what was she doing in my
mall at two in the morning?

255
00:09:53,010 --> 00:09:54,870
I don't remember falling asleep.

256
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:56,322
I remember waking up.

257
00:09:56,342 --> 00:10:00,710
Gray light was coming down through
the skylights, and the mall was quiet.

258
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:01,900
Really quiet.

259
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:04,450
I waited an hour before I moved at all.

260
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:07,850
Then I crawled to the gate,
an inch at a time, to look.

261
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:11,120
Out in the hallway, the dead
were just standing there.

262
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:13,130
Like they were waiting
for the stores to open.

263
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:15,430
In the daylight, it all looked fake.

264
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:17,930
Like a movie set somebody
forgot to take down.

265
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:21,450
Then, way off down the
hall, I heard a gate rattle.

266
00:10:21,470 --> 00:10:23,510
Then another one. Closer.

267
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:24,880
It was Marcus.

268
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:29,560
He was walking the loop, shaking every
gate he passed, the way he did every single

269
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:30,240
night.

270
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:31,370
He shook mine too.

271
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:32,520
He didn't even look in.

272
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:36,980
Down by the escalator, the night cleaner
from the food court was still pushing her

273
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,730
cart into the same wall. Over and over.

274
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:42,290
That's when I figured out the first rule.

275
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:43,710
They follow sound.

276
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:47,930
And when it's quiet, they go
back to whatever they used to do.

277
00:10:48,350 --> 00:10:49,550
I had breakfast.

278
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:52,570
A protein bar that tasted like a yoga mat.

279
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:59,120
Summit Outdoors by daylight: tents,
kayaks, a whole wall of camping food, and a

280
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:01,290
cardboard bear that scared me twice.

281
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:06,073
Then my phone buzzed. One
message, sent at two-fourteen a.m.

282
00:11:06,970 --> 00:11:08,430
It was from my mom.

283
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:10,330
Lily is with me.

284
00:11:10,540 --> 00:11:11,590
Door locked.

285
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:13,730
Do not come.

286
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:16,530
And she typed the word
not in all capitals.

287
00:11:16,610 --> 00:11:20,610
My mom is seventy, and she's the
toughest person I've ever met.

288
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:21,780
I texted back.

289
00:11:22,180 --> 00:11:23,180
Stay put.

290
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:24,440
I love you.

291
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:26,930
Tell Lily the king's still in his castle.

292
00:11:27,105 --> 00:11:28,590
It said delivered.

293
00:11:28,660 --> 00:11:31,990
Then the bars on my phone went to
zero, and they never came back.

294
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:36,450
There's a camping radio in the
store, the kind with a hand crank.

295
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:38,190
I found the emergency channel.

296
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:40,490
[RADIO] This is an emergency broadcast.

297
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:41,960
Shelter in place.

298
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,690
Rescue teams will reach every
area within thirty days.

299
00:11:45,730 --> 00:11:49,690
It played the same thirty
seconds, over and over, all day.

300
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:51,360
Thirty days.

301
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:52,260
Okay.

302
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:53,430
I can do thirty days.

303
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:55,110
I've done diets longer than that.

304
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:57,070
So I counted what I had.

305
00:11:57,262 --> 00:11:58,810
Seventy-two bottles of water.

306
00:11:58,980 --> 00:12:00,190
Forty protein bars.

307
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:05,540
A shelf of freeze-dried meals, a camp
stove, water filters, and more baseball bats

308
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:06,950
than anybody needs.

309
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:11,170
If I'm careful, that's
water for about twelve days.

310
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:14,650
Then I remembered something,
and my stomach dropped.

311
00:12:15,050 --> 00:12:16,530
My mom is diabetic.

312
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:19,250
Her insulin lasts about ten days.

313
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:21,990
Rescue was thirty days away.

314
00:12:22,120 --> 00:12:24,428
My mom's insulin would run out in ten.

315
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:29,790
There's a ladder in the stockroom
that goes up to a hatch on the roof.

316
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,150
Six years I've worked here, and
I'd never once been up there.

317
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:37,010
From the roof, you can
see the whole highway.

318
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:39,030
The sun was out.

319
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:40,040
That felt kind of rude.

320
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:44,450
Eight lanes of cars, all
stopped, doors hanging open.

321
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:48,510
And the dead, walking between them like
they'd forgotten where they parked.

322
00:12:48,620 --> 00:12:50,170
Smoke over downtown.

323
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:53,610
A helicopter that flew right
over me and didn't stop.

324
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:55,610
I waved at it like an idiot.

325
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:56,760
Both arms.

326
00:12:56,840 --> 00:13:01,800
On the roof of the hotel next door,
somebody had painted help on a bedsheet.

327
00:13:01,820 --> 00:13:03,050
Nobody came for them either.

328
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:09,110
And on the other side of all of it,
a mile and a half away, Parkview.

329
00:13:09,240 --> 00:13:12,530
Tenth floor, fourth window from the left.

330
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:15,450
I could see my daughter's window
from the roof of this mall.

331
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:16,950
It might as well have been the moon.

332
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:18,390
I know what you're thinking.

333
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:19,580
Just go.

334
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,700
My car is in the garage, with them.

335
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:26,530
There are eight lanes of the dead
between me and that building.

336
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:30,690
And if I somehow made it, I'd be leading
every one of them straight to her door.

337
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:34,630
So I stayed up there, and I
watched her window all afternoon.

338
00:13:34,650 --> 00:13:37,590
I counted every window
on that building twice.

339
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:41,010
At eight fifty-nine, the
whole city went dark.

340
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:42,250
Including her window.

341
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:44,570
No streetlights.

342
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:45,800
No windows.

343
00:13:45,820 --> 00:13:48,630
I could barely see the shape of
her building against the sky.

344
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:53,850
At nine, I turned on my flashlight and
pointed it at her building, just in case.

345
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:56,110
Nine o'clock. Nothing.

346
00:13:56,732 --> 00:13:58,650
Nine-oh-one. Nothing.

347
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:03,800
I stood there with my flashlight, telling
myself the power's out, she can't, it's

348
00:14:03,820 --> 00:14:04,700
fine.

349
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:07,911
Then, one. Two. Three.

350
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:08,960
Not her lamp.

351
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:10,190
A flashlight.

352
00:14:10,210 --> 00:14:11,690
In the tenth-floor window.

353
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:12,940
She's alive.

354
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:13,940
She figured it out.

355
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:16,510
She's seven years old,
and she figured it out.

356
00:14:16,620 --> 00:14:18,730
I blinked back three times.

357
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:21,710
Then I sat down on the roof,
and I couldn't stop shaking.

358
00:14:21,897 --> 00:14:23,700
Then the radio in my pocket clicked on.

359
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:25,070
Marcus's radio.

360
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:26,690
I'd carried it around all day.

361
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:28,690
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] That's sweet, Danny.

362
00:14:29,180 --> 00:14:30,350
She'd been watching.

363
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:31,950
[WOMAN ON THE RADIO] Who's in the window?
